<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Ascania</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ascanians</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Ascania is a German princely dynasty named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aschersleben">Aschersleben</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-the-bear">Albert the Bear</a> founded the Margraviate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> in 1157, and his descendants ruled it until the line died out in 1320, extending German settlement east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>. Other branches held the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and Anhalt, where they ruled until 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-ascania.jpg" alt="House of Ascania" width="320" height="368" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Glasshouse with elements by Sodacan and Trondivers · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_Arms_of_the_Duchy_of_Anhalt.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The House of Ascania is a princely family documented since the 11th century, also known as the House of Anhalt after its longest-held possession. The name Askanier is a Latinization of its seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aschersleben">Aschersleben</a>. The family appears first as the Counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ballenstedt">Ballenstedt</a>, from an area now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a> district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>, with Ballenstedt as the eponymous seat in the 11th and 12th centuries and Aschersleben and finally Anhalt Castle among the most significant seats. Prince Bernhard III of Anhalt was the first, in 1320, to call himself comes Ascanie, and nineteenth-century historiography applied that term to all members of the family since the 11th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-the-bear">Albert the Bear</a> became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-saxony">Duke of Saxony</a> in 1138 and was the first margrave in the formerly Slavic settlement area of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>. When Pribislav died in 1150 his widow Petrissa handed the land of the Hevelli around Brandenburg Castle to Albert, who took definitive possession in 1157. In 1180 the eastern parts of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> passed to Bernhard of Saxony, and as Dukes of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a> the family received hereditary electoral dignity in 1356.</p><p>After Bernhard III, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-saxony">Duke of Saxony</a>, died in February 1212, his son Henry received the Anhalt house estates between the Lower Harz and the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mulde">Mulde</a>, while his brother Albert inherited the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. The division marks the beginning of Anhalt's independent development, with Henry I as its first ruler and the first to be called prince in Anhalt. Henry II founded the Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aschersleben">Aschersleben</a> line in 1252, and in 1603 another division created the younger lines of Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a>, Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernburg">Bernburg</a>, Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kothen">Köthen</a> and Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zerbst">Zerbst</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Alexius Friedrich Christian of Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernburg">Bernburg</a> was the first Anhalt prince to gain the title of duke, in April 1806. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zerbst">Zerbst</a> line ceased in 1793 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kothen">Köthen</a> line in 1847, and when the Bernburg line died out in 1863 the Duchy of Anhalt was formed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-iv-duke-of-anhalt">Leopold IV Frederick</a>. It sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1866, joined the North German Confederation in 1867 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> in 1871. Aribert of Anhalt abdicated in 1918, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-revolution-of-1918-1919">November Revolution</a>. Leopold III of Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a> had created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau-worlitz-garden-realm">Wörlitz Park</a>, an English landscape garden. Since the late 17th century only the Anhalt branch has survived.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Ascania” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1134 Emperor Lothair III gave the Northern March to Albert the Bear of the <b>House of Ascania</b>; Albert had an understanding with Pribislav-Henry, the Christian prince of the Hevelli, who made him his heir, and on Pribislav’s death in 1150 he took over the fortress of Brandenburg…</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
